  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does adrenaline do?",
      "a": "Adrenaline prepares the body for sudden action. It speeds up the heart, widens the airways, sends more blood to the muscles, releases sugar from the liver for fuel, and slows down digestion.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the fight or flight hormone?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where is adrenaline made?",
      "a": "In the adrenal glands, which sit on top of the kidneys. Their inner part, the adrenal medulla, releases adrenaline when nerves from the brain signal danger or excitement.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the adrenal glands"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does your heart pound when you are scared?",
      "a": "Adrenaline and the nerves of the fight-or-flight system make the heart beat faster and more strongly, pumping more blood and oxygen to your muscles in case you need to move fast.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the heart",
        "Why does fear make my heart race?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do you feel shaky after a fright?",
      "a": "Adrenaline keeps muscles tense and ready, and extra sugar and a fast heartbeat linger for a while. As it wears off, many people feel shaky or tired.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do my hands shake when I am nervous?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How fast does adrenaline work?",
      "a": "Very fast: it is released within seconds of a scare and acts throughout the body almost at once, because it travels in the blood.",
      "asks": [
        "How quick is the adrenaline rush?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Adrenaline, also called epinephrine, is made in the adrenal medulla, the inner part of the adrenal glands.",
    "The adrenal glands sit on top of the kidneys."
  ],
